menu "RV-9"

config RV9_KERNEL_NATIVE
    bool "Back the KAL with RV-9's own kernel"
    default n
    help
        Selects which kernel RV-9 runs on.

        Off: the KAL is implemented on FreeRTOS, which is how RV-9 has run
        since phase 0 and remains the reference.

        On: the KAL is implemented on RV-9's own scheduler, and the whole
        system -- processes, I/O, the shell -- is scheduled by it. FreeRTOS
        is still underneath holding one task, which is where RV-9's kernel
        runs; taking the machine outright is phase 7 step 3.

        Both are built from the same sources above the KAL and pass the
        same conformance suite.

config RV9_HEAP_FLOOR
    int "Heap RV-9 will not take (bytes)"
    default 12288
    help
        A reserve that ordinary allocation is refused out of.

        The floor is not for RV-9's benefit. It is for everything RV-9
        cannot ask: WiFi, the PHY, the SPI driver and ESP-IDF's own
        internals allocate straight from the heap and abort when they
        cannot get what they need -- inside a layer RV-9 does not own,
        which on a vehicle means the whole system stopping because a
        display wanted a buffer.

        Allocations that go through the KAL can be told no. So the last
        few kilobytes are simply never offered to them: a request that
        would take free memory below this returns NULL, a process fails
        to start and says so, and the machine stays up.

        Zero disables it, which is how RV-9 behaved before phase 4.

endmenu
